    verdict: KeyOmitsAnInputTheValueDependsOn {
      undeclared_input: "the RESOLVING CONTEXT, inherited one level up and with LESS to look at. This producer is nullary, so its args_hash is EMPTY and its key is the declaration alone -- while its whole value is a fold over coproduct_arm_keys(type_name: affected_set_reflect_gate_type). An empty key over a ctx-dependent value is the same defect as the row above with nothing in the key to inspect" as String,
    },
    carrier_modules: ["v2.test.claim.affected_set_universe"],
    measurement: concat("required floor run 34742966153, artifact required-floor-cross-claim-demand, the row keyed producer=ci_gate_processes arg_shape=keyed, decl_site src/v2/lens/affected_set.dag; its cost and module columns are read from that artifact rather than copied here. arg_shape reads `keyed` because a nullary call has zero unkeyable arguments, which is exactly the point: the census cannot distinguish `every input is in the key` from `there are no inputs in the key`. that row reports a SINGLE consumer module today, so no cross-scope serve is observed for THIS producer -- the refusal is that nothing prevents one and the key could not express the constraint if it did ", homonym_census_note()),
    next_trigger: "the same capability as the row above: reflection keyed by qualified or scoped identity. This producer has no repair of its own -- it is sound exactly when its callee is" as String
  },
  RefusedShareCandidate {
    producer: "v2.lens.affected_set.meta_processes",
    verdict: KeyOmitsAnInputTheValueDependsOn {
      undeclared_input: "the RESOLVING CONTEXT, by the same route as ci_gate_processes: nullary, empty key, value folded over coproduct_arm_keys(type_name: affected_set_reflect_meta_process_kind_type)" as String,
    },
    carrier_modules: ["v2.test.claim.affected_set_universe"],
    measurement: "THIS ROW IS A WITHDRAWAL OF AN ENROLMENT THIS LANE PROPOSED, recorded as such rather than as a candidate that was never admitted. gunbc#11247 proposed carrying this producer WARM on a real duplication measurement -- the floor own census carried a producer=meta_processes row whose cross-claim share was a visible fraction of its total, and the carry demonstrably worked: with it applied, affected_set_universe_includes_meta_self drops to a SMALL FRACTION of its unserved step count and meta_processes DISAPPEARS from the cross-claim census of run 34742966153 because it is served rather than re-derived. The cost case was correct and is not what refuses it. NO FIGURE FROM THAT MEASUREMENT IS TRANSCRIBED HERE, and the withdrawal does not need one: what is preserved is that a real duplication was measured and that the cost case was sound, both re-derivable from the named run and artifact. What refuses it is the key: the serve-boundary check made when it was proposed asked whether the VALUE was portable (no Closure, no OriginBoundNode at any depth) and never asked whether the KEY covered every input. It does not" as String,
    next_trigger: "the same capability: reflection keyed by qualified or scoped identity. When it lands this refused row is deleted and the derived share admits the producer wherever its planned demand qualifies, with the receipt gunbc#11247 already measured, which is why that measurement is preserved above rather than discarded with the enrolment" as String
  }
]

// Folded rather than mapped, and with no new import: `fold`, `concat`, `reverse` and `any`
// resolve as kernel builtins here exactly as they do in `v2.workflow.floor_changed_witness`.
// This module is a DECLARED CLOSURE SEED of the required floor
// (`REQUIRED_FLOOR_RUNTIME_AUTHORITY_MODULES`), so every import it grows widens the subject every
// required run must frame; a wall that costs the floor a new module edge to express is a worse
// trade than one spelled in the kernel.
fn refused_share_producers(refused: List<RefusedShareCandidate>) -> List<String> {
  reverse(fold(refused, init: [], f: fn(acc, row) { concat([row.producer], acc) }))
}

// WHICH REFUSALS TRANSFER THROUGH CARRIERS, AND WHICH ARE ABOUT ONE SPELLING ONLY. This is the
// authority for the overlap wall's population, and the executor mirrors it; it is declared here
// because it is a fact about what each VERDICT MEANS, and the verdicts are this roster's.
//
// The overlap wall's premise is that a later identity reaching a refused row's measured
// neighbourhood INHERITS the measured effect. That premise has content only where an effect was
// measured. `MeasuredServeAboveRecompute` is exactly that case -- serving cost real, over those
// consumers -- and it is the case this file's own history produced twice, through two identities.
//
// `NoMeasuredEffectOverItsConsumers` has NOTHING TO INHERIT: the row was refused because serving
// it neither cost nor saved anything over those modules. Refusing a different admitted producer
// for touching one of them would charge it with a harm measured to be zero, which is a fabricated
// subject, and this is not hypothetical -- run 33696651737 refused
// v2.compiler.target_serialize.grammar_relation_row_for_emitted on exactly that overlap, and the same
// overlap is present in the main-vintage run 33671317370, so it is a standing property of the
// corpus rather than one run's accident.
//
// `SupersededBySingleAuthorityRepair` is a DESIGN section 3 ruling about one spelling, not a cost
// that travels with consumers; the repair takes the series as an argument and other producers
// serving the same witnesses inherit nothing from it.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED BY THIS PREDICATE, stated because the same run that motivated it could be
// misread as clearing the producer it refused. Nothing here says
// v2.compiler.target_serialize.grammar_relation_row_for_emitted earns its enrolment. The core_edges
// measurement measures a DIFFERENT producer and says nothing about that key's own serving cost
// over v2.test.manual.rust_add_emit_translate; what it establishes is only that the overlap is
// not EVIDENCE of a cost, because the row overlapped measured none. Whether that key beats
// recompute over its own consumers is the question this roster's ordinary admission discipline
// already owns, answered present-vs-absent like every other row, and it is open.
//
// SO THE NARROWING IS TO A VERDICT, NOT TO AN IDENTITY. Both rows the inheritance actually
// happened through are `MeasuredServeAboveRecompute` and both stay in the wall's population. The
// two arms that leave it are still refused by the identity fold above on their own spelling, so
// no row loses coverage of its own re-proposal -- what they lose is a claim over OTHER producers
// that the measurement never supported.
// `KeyOmitsAnInputTheValueDependsOn` DOES NOT TRANSFER, and that is the one arm worth saying why
// about. `MeasuredServeAboveRecompute` travels because it is a measured cost fact about a SHAPE that
// other producers share. A key objection is a fact about ONE producer's key, so transferring it
// would refuse unrelated candidates on an objection that does not apply to them -- the inflation the
// failure-mode row above warns about, in the other direction.
fn refused_row_carriers_transfer(verdict: ShareRefusalVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    MeasuredServeAboveRecompute => true
    NoMeasuredEffectOverItsConsumers => false
    SupersededBySingleAuthorityRepair { repaired_by: _ } => false
    KeyOmitsAnInputTheValueDependsOn { undeclared_input: _ } => false
  }
}

// THIS WALL IS AT IDENTITY GRAIN AND IDENTITY GRAIN IS NOT SUFFICIENT. Stated here rather than
// discovered later, because this file already contains the case it misses.
//
// `rust_target_model_staging` above was refused for the SAME MEASURED EFFECT as
// `rust_target_model` and is a DIFFERENT IDENTITY. When the two were enrolled together its
// consumer set was 17 modules and did not include the produced-decl cluster; it became 44
// modules including that cluster only on the run where the first row was withdrawn and staging
// INHERITED its consumers. Two identities, one effect, and the fold above is green on that every
// time.
//
// SO THE WALL THAT CATCHES WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED IS AN OVERLAP JOIN AGAINST `carrier_modules`,
// AND IT DOES NOT BELONG IN THIS FILE. A proposal's consumer set is not declared anywhere and is
// not derivable from a roster of qualified names; it exists in the run's own `[floor-shared-fill]`
// ledger. Making the author declare it here would mint a field nothing verifies -- every green
// witness an author writing literals -- and the staging case shows that form would also have been
// STALE-CORRECT: a set written at enrolment time was accurate when authored and wrong at the
// moment the inheritance happened.
//
// THAT CAPABILITY IS THE SECOND WALL, AND IT LANDS WITH THIS ONE. The required-floor executor
// reads this roster and, once the fold is over and the shared-fill ledger is final, joins every
// observed cross-claim share key's OBSERVED consumer modules against these `carrier_modules` and
// refuses on overlap -- `refuse_pure_producer_share_refused_carrier_overlap` in
// `v1_compiler::cli_run::required_floor_runner`, called immediately after the ledger is rendered
// so the refusal cites two lines the operator can already see above it. It is in the seed rather
// than here for the reason above: the population it adjudicates is observed, not declarable.
//
// ITS REFUSAL NAMES THE SUBJECT, BECAUSE IT DOES NOT FIRE ON THE RUN THAT CAUSED IT. An overlap
// is created by the diff that WITHDRAWS a wide row and hands its consumers to a narrower one, so
// the red lands on the withdrawer, not on whoever proposed the surviving key. Charging a cost to
// a principal that did not cause it is DESIGN section 5 externalization unless the transfer is
// visible, so the diagnostic names three things: the admitted key that now overlaps, the refused
// row it inherited the carriers from, and -- in its own sentence -- that what changed is the
// roster and not that key's own cost.
//
// AND THE CLASS'S RUNG IS THE MINIMUM ACROSS ITS PATHS, NOT THE STRONGER WALL'S. DESIGN section
// 4b(1) is explicit that a class's current rung is the minimum over its in-scope paths. The class
// is "a producer this roster has measured and refused is re-admitted", and it has two paths:
// literal re-proposal of the same spelling, which `derive_cross_claim_share` declines by construction (its
// `RefusedByMeasurement` arm), and re-admission through a second identity that inherits the refused row's
// consumers, which the executor refuses at RUNTIME over one run's observed ledger. A runtime
// refusal is rung 2, mechanically preventable -- the invalid state stays writable and safety
// depends on the required floor executing and staying enrolled. So the sentence to carry out of
// this file is that the class is MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, not that it is structural; the fold's
// stronger rung is one path's, and citing it as the class's would be inflation.
//
// NEXT TRIGGER, naming the capability: a consumer set that is DERIVABLE rather than observed --
// the modeled effect/realization facts that would let a producer's consumers be computed from
// the graph instead of read off a run. That, and only that, moves the second path from a runtime
// refusal to a structural one; a second observed-ledger check would not.
//
// THE LITERAL PATH IS NOW CONSTRUCTION, NOT A WALL. The fold that refused an admitted roster row
// naming a refused producer is deleted with the roster: `derive_cross_claim_share` declines every
// closed demand whose producer a refused row names (`RefusedByMeasurement`), so a refused producer
// cannot be admitted by its own spelling. The executor's overlap check above still guards the
// second path, re-admission through another identity reaching the refused row's carriers.

// ── A THIRD FILL DISPOSITION: A PREPARED EFFECT INPUT, CARRIED ────────────────────────────
//
// THE ROW KIND THE DERIVATION CANNOT PRODUCE. A derived row is a pure call with a closed argument
// row, filled by the first planned claim that evaluates it. A producer whose value depends on ONE
// READ OF A COMMITTED CARRIER fits neither, and the reason is in the KEY rather than in
// anyone's taste: a stored entry is keyed on (resolved fn node, portable ARGUMENT ROW), and a
// nullary call that reaches a read contributes NOTHING about what it read to that key. Warming
// it would let two different carrier contents share one entry — the content form of the
// ctx-blind homonym class this file already refuses by resolving every spelling to a node. So
// such a producer could only ever be CLAIM-FORCED, where it re-derives once per claim frame:
// the rostered class `shared_precondition_re_derived_once_per_claim_frame`.
//
// THE LIVE INSTANCE. `gunbc.roadmap_authority.roadmap_authority_projection` is one
// `Filesystem.Read` of `dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_acceptance_event_history.jsonl` followed by a
// pure fold, demanded by seven required-lane claims and by gunbc#10994 — whose merge path is
// blocked by an enrolment margin on exactly this shared precondition.
//
// WHY CARRYING IT IS BINDING AN INPUT AND NOT CACHING AN EFFECT, which is the whole licence for
// this row kind. Hermetic evaluation ALREADY classifies this read as INPUT ACCESS: the
// checkout-input carve-out in `v1_interpreter::eval_service_call` dispatches a readonly
// `Filesystem.Read`/`List` wet when `hermetic_checkout_input_disposition` confirms the path
// under the checkout root, and refuses — typed and located — everything it cannot confirm (an
// out-of-root path, a `.git`/`target` component, an unresolvable path). The value is therefore a
// function of the commit the run prepared, constant for the whole prepared subject, and
// ACQUIRING IT ONCE IS BINDING AN INPUT THE RUN ALREADY HOLDS FIXED. A producer reaching host
// state that wall cannot confirm is never carryable, because that wall refuses it first.
//
// WHAT PREPARATION DOES, and every arm of it stops the line rather than widening:
//   1. resolve each acquisition to its fn node in a frame over the prepared subject;
//   2. refuse any acquisition that is not nullary — a carried input WITH arguments is a
//      different concept and must not arrive under this one;
//   3. evaluate it ONCE under the shared-build observation, so it is billed to preparation and
//      adjudicated by the same preparation refusal as every other shared build;
//   4. reify the value TOTALLY to its portable form — the same total walk publication already
//      performs — and take its structural digest as the carried CONTENT IDENTITY;
//   5. bind it for the prepared subject's lifetime, cleared with the tier.
//
// NO STALE SERVE IS UNWRITABLE RATHER THAN GUARDED. The carried value IS the key material: a
// changed carrier content produces a different portable argument row, the stored row fails to
// match, and the producer recomputes. There is no staleness check to forget to run, which is
// DESIGN section 5's construction-over-validation in the one place a cache usually gets it
// backwards.
//
// THE FLOOR NEVER READS A DOMAIN TYPE. When an acquisition SUCCEEDS and returns its own
// coproduct's refusal arm (`RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused` and its peers), the
// carried value is that refusal, faithfully, and every claim receives the IDENTICAL typed value
// it would have computed for itself. That is propagation, not widening: no claim passes where it
// would otherwise have failed, and the alternative — the floor stopping on a named domain
// variant — would make this module enumerate `gunbc.roadmap_authority`'s vocabulary, the section
// 3 layer inversion (adjudicated 2026-09-14: fierce-lark-661 and bright-boar-435, both
// approving, the second conditioning approval on the disposition being PRINTED so a run where
// the carrier refused is diagnosable rather than silent — the floor's phase line carries the
// carried value's outermost variant name and content digest for exactly that reason).
//
// SECTION 2's LADDER, WHICH IS THE STRUCTURAL AUTHORITY HERE AND IS CITED IN SOURCE RATHER THAN
// IN A PLAN DOC. `std.materialization_ladder` is the authority this row kind answers to, and the
// answer is stated at its own vocabulary: the demand's identity is the resolved declaration; the
// obligation LCA is required-floor PREPARATION, which is the least common visible ancestor of
// the consuming claims (each claim is an isolated frame, so the carry crosses an isolation
// boundary and only a STORE tier may discharge it — `std.materialization_ladder.tier_axes`
// derives that, this module does not restate it); the provider is the cross-claim tier, a
// `MemoTier { keying: ContentKeyed }` whose coverage is the enrolled identities below and whose
// retention is released at its scope's exit with an exact byte ceiling. The section 2 ordering is
// respected in the direction the text writes it: the demand graph is MINIMIZED FIRST — the
// repetition has a shared-state least common ancestor, so it is authored duplication whose repair
// is to CARRY the first value, and the tier is merely where the carried value is held. The typed
// THE FOUR OBLIGATIONS, EACH AS A TYPED VALUE DERIVED FROM THE ROW, and deliberately NOT as four
// more String fields beside it. A row already carries its computation identity — the resolved
// declaration in `acquisition` / `producer` — and the other three are properties of the provider,
// not of the row, so copying them per row would be one fact in two places (section 3) and the
// transcription section 6 forbids. They are derived and named here instead:
//   computation identity -> the row's own `acquisition` / `producer`, consumed as the ladder
//     demand's `identity` by `floor_prepared_effect_input_ladder.acquisition_demand` and
//     `producer_demand`;
//   declared inputs      -> the carried content digest, which IS the stored key's argument row;
//     stated to the ladder as `MemoTier { keying: ContentKeyed }` and printed per run on the
//     `phase=prepared-effect-input-acquire` line as `content_digest=`;
//   provider scope       -> `floor_prepared_effect_input_ladder.floor_preparation_frame`, the
//     obligation LCA of the consuming claims — and the ladder's verdict at a SHARED-STATE LCA is
//     AuthoredDuplication rather than Discharged, which is the ladder refusing to let a provider
//     stand in for the carry: section 2's repair there is to carry the first value, so the pair
//     `every_carried_identity_is_authored_duplication_before_the_carry` /
//     `every_carried_identity_is_a_single_demand_after_the_carry` states the transition this row
//     kind performs instead of claiming a discharge the ladder would not give;
//   retention            -> `floor_prepared_effect_input_ladder.cross_claim_tier_retention`,
//     released at the provider's scope exit with the tier's exact byte ceiling.
// The conformance rows and the fold that decides them live in
// `v2.workflow.floor_prepared_effect_input_ladder`, one module out, because THIS module is a
// declared closure seed of the required floor (`REQUIRED_FLOOR_RUNTIME_AUTHORITY_MODULES`) and
// every import it grows widens the subject every required run must frame.

// HOW A WARM ROW DECLARES ITS DEPENDENCE ON A CARRIED INPUT, and there are exactly two shapes
// because there are exactly two ways a producer can reach the acquisition.
//
// `BoundParameter` — the producer TAKES the acquired value as a named argument, so a claim's own
// call already carries the content in its argument row and the stored key needs no help. This is
// the shape the roadmap projections took in gunbc#11303.
//
// `ImplicitAcquisition` — the producer is nullary and reaches the acquisition INSIDE itself. The
// carried input's content identity is folded into the key on both the warm and the serve, so the
// entry is content-keyed exactly as the parameterised shape is. This arm carries one obligation
// the other does not, and it is enforced by execution rather than by this sentence: with the
// carry installed, the producer's warm must dispatch NO effect. A producer that still reaches
// the world has a second input the key does not represent, and the floor refuses the row —
// `PreparedEffectInputProducerStillDispatchesEffect` — rather than storing a value whose key is
// incomplete. That is the one check that separates "its only input is this carrier" from "we
// hope its only input is this carrier".
type CarriedInputDependence
  = BoundParameter { parameter: String }
  | ImplicitAcquisition

// `acquisition` is the nullary declaration whose evaluation acquires the input; `checkout_input`
// is the committed path whose content IS the declared input, named so a reader can see WHAT the
// carried identity is a digest of without running the floor. `ground` states why this is a
// commit-determined input rather than host state; `measurement` names the instrument that
// re-derives the row's present-vs-absent verdict and never transcribes its output.
type PreparedEffectInput {
  acquisition: String
  checkout_input: String
  ground: String
  measurement: String
}

type CarriedInputWarmRow {
  producer: String
  carried_input: String
  dependence: CarriedInputDependence
  measurement: String
}

data floor_cross_claim_prepared_effect_inputs: List<PreparedEffectInput> = [
  PreparedEffectInput {
    acquisition: "v2.test.claim.declaring_identity_spelling.production_ingest.xl3_fixture_reads",
    checkout_input: "src/v2/test/fixture/declaring_identity_spelling/ (+ dag/test/probe/declaring_identity_spelling_unresolvable_probe.dag)",
    ground: "The NAMESPACE-XL-3 fixture corpus: eight committed .dag files read through filesystem_read into one list of read witnesses; each census is STILL a single-file ingest over its own witness (the census semantics are per file), the acquisition only performs the reads once. Content fixed by the commit the run prepared. Demanded by every claim of v2.test.claim.declaring_identity_spelling.production_ingest through xl3_fixture_censuses.",
    measurement: "The floor's own refusal on gunbc#11461 run 35074340220 is the absent arm: fifteen claims of that file COMPLETED-OVER-COST-REQUIREMENT at 130k-430k eval steps against the new-witness budget, each re-deriving its own parse-and-normalize. The present arm is the same run shape with these rows enrolled, read over required_floor_claim_cost.tsv and the [floor-shared-fill] ledger; the acquisition's own arm is the effect-dispatch count for this carrier - eight reads at preparation with the row enrolled, eight per consuming claim without it."
  },
  PreparedEffectInput {
    acquisition: "v2.test.claim.reference_derived_graph_fixture.fixture_ingest",
    checkout_input: "dag/test/fixture/reference_derived_graph/",
    ground: "The NAMESPACE-XL-4 fixture corpus: eleven committed .dag modules under the checkout root, read through filesystem_read into one SourceRootIngest so the reference-derived graph claims measure the SAME files the instrument tools.reference_derived_graph reads (one fixture authority - review 65417 on gunbc#11202). Their content is fixed by the commit the run prepared, which is what makes one acquisition at preparation the same ingest every claim would have read for itself. Demanded by v2.test.claim.reference_derived_graph_call_arg_tree and reference_derived_graph_production_ingest through xl4_fixture_trees.",
    measurement: "The floor's own refusal on gunbc#11461 run 35059914771 (PureProducerShareWarmDispatchedEffect producer=xl4_fixture_trees effects=11) is the absent arm: the warm reached the world eleven times. The present arm is the same run shape with this row enrolled, read over required_floor_claim_cost.tsv and the [floor-shared-fill] ledger; the acquisition's own arm is the effect-dispatch count for this carrier, which is one prepared acquisition with the row enrolled and eleven inside the warm without it."
  },
  PreparedEffectInput {
    acquisition: "test.claim.legacy_repair_tap_live_bridge_witness.xl1_fixture_root_tap",
    checkout_input: "dag/test/fixture/reference_derived_graph/ (primary root) + dag/std/ (dependency pool) + tools/whole_corpus_compile_measured_root_demands.json (the root's admission row)",
    ground: "The NAMESPACE-XL-1 live tap over its measured fixture root: the seed builtin emit_rust_reference_derived_rows_bridge compiles the eleven committed fixture modules against the dag/std pool and returns the Observed encoding (repair rows, occurrence transport, binding rows). Every input is under the checkout root and fixed by the commit the run prepared - the sources, and the demand projection whose row admits the root - so one acquisition at preparation is the same value each of the two Observed-arm claims of that module would have compiled for itself. Demanded by a_measured_fixture_root_reaches_the_observed_arm_with_rows_and_bindings and the_live_census_over_the_measured_fixture_root_counts_its_rows. THE MODULE IS UNDER dag/test/claim/**, OUTSIDE THE GATE CLOSURE, AND THAT IS ADMITTED BY gunbc#11455 RATHER THAN BY THE LIVE-DEPLOY ARGUMENT ABOVE: roster admission is scoped to the prepared subject, and a rostered module present in the corpus but outside the subject is counted and not evaluated (required_floor_runner disposition 2) - so this acquisition runs exactly when its two consumers are planned, which is when this witness module is in the subject, and is never demanded by a subject that does not carry it.",
    measurement: "Absent arm: claim_batch --entry dag/test/claim/legacy_repair_tap_live_bridge_witness_test.dag on a session host records each of those two claims paying the compile itself (the [witness] lines carry seconds of cpu against the floor's 500 ms per-claim ceiling), and before this row's root was measured the floor refused the arm at admission in microseconds (gunbc#11461 runs 35074340220, 35079253040). Present arm: the same run shape with this row enrolled, read over required_floor_claim_cost.tsv and the [floor-shared-fill] ledger, the acquisition landing once at preparation with disposition=Stored and both claims serving hits."
  },
  PreparedEffectInput {
    acquisition: "gunbc.roadmap_authority.roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load",
    checkout_input: "dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_acceptance_event_history.jsonl",
    ground: "A committed carrier under the checkout root, so hermetic evaluation already reads it as INPUT ACCESS rather than a host effect (v1_interpreter hermetic_checkout_input_disposition). Its content is fixed by the commit the run prepared, which is what makes one acquisition at preparation the same value every claim would have read for itself. Demanded by every claim that reaches the roadmap authority: the seven required-lane roadmap claims and gunbc#10994's forecast witness.",
    measurement: "Present-vs-absent on this row, both arms by workflow_dispatch on a branch ref pinned to an exact sha per this module's corrected procedure, read over required_floor_claim_cost.tsv and the run's own [floor-shared-fill] ledger; the acquisition's own arm is the run's effect-dispatch count for this carrier, which is 1 with the row enrolled and one-per-consuming-claim without it."
  }
